    "result_text": "Done. Tests are green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 passing).\n\n**Finding addressed \u2014 I agree it was valid.** The inline `\"NSW\".equals(locality) || \"VIC\".equals(locality) || \"QLD\".equals(locality)` was a fourth parallel copy of the known-region set, which is exactly the drift the codebase's \"single source of truth\" convention (`CityRegions`, `PostcodeRules`, `RegionTimezones`) exists to prevent.\n\nI took the simpler of the two suggested fixes:\n\n```java\nString area = \"UNKNOWN\".equals(toLocality(owner)) ? \"REGIONAL\" : \"METRO\";\n```\n\n`toLocality(owner)` is already the single resolution point \u2014 it maps every city/postcode not in the central region tables to the `\"UNKNOWN\"` sentinel. So \"known region\" is now tested once, at that resolution point, and a region added or renamed in the central tables flows through automatically and stays in lock-step with the `locality` and `timezone` fields. I also updated the Javadoc to describe this derivation rather than re-listing `NSW/VIC/QLD`.\n\nI considered the alternative (a shared `RegionTimezones.isKnownRegion` predicate) but the sentinel approach is less code and ties the segment directly to the same value the spec phrases the rule against (\"when the locality is a known region\"), so it reads most faithfully here.",
