    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe logic placement is otherwise idiomatic \u2014 a `toOwnerSegment(Owner)` default method sitting alongside its `toX(owner)` siblings, wired through an `@Mapping(... expression)`, with a matching Javadoc and openapi schema. The tier/area composition is clear. One structural issue:\n\n**The known-region set `{NSW, VIC, QLD}` is re-enumerated inline.** Line 116:\n\n```java\nboolean metro = \"NSW\".equals(locality) || \"VIC\".equals(locality) || \"QLD\".equals(locality);\n```\n\nThis hardcodes a fourth parallel copy of the set of known regions. The codebase deliberately centralizes this knowledge and each holder documents it as the single source of truth so \"a region added or renamed here is reflected everywhere at once\":\n- `CityRegions.CITY_REGION` (Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD)\n- `PostcodeRules.REGION_POSTCODES` (NSW/VIC/QLD ranges)\n- `RegionTimezones.REGION_TIMEZONE` (NSW/VIC/QLD \u2192 timezone)\n\nA new inline `equals` disjunction is exactly the scattered, drift-prone region knowledge this convention exists to prevent \u2014 add a region (say TAS) tomorrow and this one method silently keeps classifying it as `REGIONAL`, out of step with `locality` and `timezone`.\n\nSuggested fixes (either is in keeping with the surrounding code):\n- Simplest: `toLocality` already collapses every non-known region to the `\"UNKNOWN\"` sentinel, so the membership test can lean on that single resolution point rather than re-listing the regions:\n  ```java\n  boolean metro = !\"UNKNOWN\".equals(locality);\n  ```\n- Or, if an explicit membership predicate reads better, source it from a shared table \u2014 e.g. a `RegionTimezones.isKnownRegion(locality)` (a region with a mapped timezone) or a dedicated known-regions set \u2014 so the \"known region\" set stays defined in one place, consistent with the three utilities above.",
